 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Memory Bandwidth:** The method aims to reduce memory bandwidth usage by increasing the ratio of memory bandwidth to voxel count through multiresolution representation [S1].
-- **Performance:** The Multi-Resolution MIP (MMIP) algorithm allows for straightforward load spreading over multiple graphics cards, achieving almost optimal speedup for large volumes [S1].
-- **Hardware Requirements:** Requires graphics hardware capable of vertex shaders and multiple GPU support (e.g., newer NVIDIA drivers supporting multiple cards for one X-server) [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Efficient handling of large volumetric datasets; supports interactive adjustment of orientation and zoom; enables multi-GPU acceleration [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Relies on orthogonal projection in the described examples (though generality is claimed) [S1]. The specific compositing details for combining levels are not provided in the source [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of non-zero voxels across all levels, $O(N_{non-zero})$, as only active voxels are projected [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for $L+1$ volumes, increasing memory usage compared to single-resolution rendering [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Offers significant gain in the ratio of memory bandwidth to voxel count by reducing the number of processed voxels [S1].
+*   **Scalability:** Supports load balancing across multiple graphics cards by splitting the dataset and rendering to separate screens, then combining results [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** High speed for large volumes due to hardware acceleration of point projection and multiresolution culling [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires pre-computation of wavelet decomposition; orthogonal projection assumption in basic examples may limit perspective accuracy without modification [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method is described in the paper "Multiresolution MIP Rendering of Large Volumetric Data Accelerated on Graphics Hardware" (EuroVis 2007) [S1]. It builds on the principles of multiresolution and level of detail, specifically utilizing wavelet-based decomposition for volume rendering [S1, S2]. The approach was developed to address the computational and memory challenges of rendering large volumetric datasets on graphics hardware [S1].
+The method was introduced in the context of multiresolution MIP rendering of large volumetric data accelerated on graphics hardware [S1]. It builds on the principles of wavelet-based multiresolution decomposition, specifically leveraging the separation of detail coefficients to optimize rendering pipelines [S1]. The approach was developed to address the memory bandwidth bottleneck in volume rendering by reducing the effective voxel count processed per frame [S1].
 
